# Source files stay small: 300 warn, 400 red zone, 500 split now

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 convention.

## Use This When

- A file you edit is at or past 300 lines, or you are about to add code to one.
- `bun run refactor:ci` fails in `bun run check` or in the pre-commit hook.

## Stop Here If

- The file is under 300 lines, or it is a baselined file that did not grow.

## Core rule

- Tiers by line count of tracked source files (tests, declaration files, generated modules and fixtures excluded):
  - 300 lines, warn: getting hard to read in one sitting; split it when you next touch it.
  - 400 lines, red zone: the file may not grow any further.
  - 500 lines, split now: it carries several responsibilities.
- The gate `bun run refactor:ci` (part of `bun run check` and of the pre-commit hook) is a ratchet over `refactor-baseline.json`, a machine-local, gitignored file like the screenshot baselines: the first run on a checkout writes it from the sizes committed where HEAD left `main` (their merge base), never from the working tree, so a fresh checkout, a worktree included, starts from main's ceiling at its first commit. A file at or above 400 lines the baseline does not know fails; a baselined file that grew fails; shrinking is always allowed, and `bun run refactor:baseline` records the smaller size. The baseline only shrinks; raising an entry needs `--allow-growth` and an owner decision on the card. Never commit the baseline.
- Split by responsibility, never by size alone: one file, one reason to change. A view past the limit becomes a parent with child views; a service holding two workflows becomes two services behind the same port; a protocol double with one switch per message becomes one handler module per message group; a composition file that opens, syncs and signs out becomes one module per lifecycle step.
- Never split into a `utils` or `helpers` dump. Each new file gets a name that says what it owns, lives in the layer it belongs to, and a module README line when it changes a module's surface. One class or one business function per file (`one-unit-per-file.mdc`); only tiny related utilities share a topic-named file.
- `bun run refactor:scan` lists every file past the warn tier, largest first, with its baseline where it has one.
